Cell["8. Implementation notes", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"672c84ff-8c83-4b85-af70-6335686317f3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\[Bullet] ",
 "Only the diffusion is done in Fourier space; advection uses NDSolveValue's \
RK45 stepper over the reconstructed velocity. Modes below a relative \
tolerance (",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["10", 
    RowBox[{"-", "3"}]], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Times",ExpressionUUID->
  "e541e4b4-5534-490f-8d03-d607b9b8f641"],
 " by default) are considered extinct, giving ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["t", "\[Star]"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Times",ExpressionUUID->
  "3801d1cf-421b-4523-b308-cc8a0337b1b1"],
 ".\n\[Bullet] ",
 "Speed mode replaces the per-sub-step inverse DCT with a linear \
interpolation between 60 log-spaced density/gradient snapshots, giving \
sub-pixel agreement with Quality at ~7\[Times] the throughput. Switch to ",
 StyleBox["PerformanceGoal -> \"Quality\"", "Input"],
 " when you need bit-exact reproduction.\n\[Bullet] ",
 "Heavy-tailed metrics (Monaco on population density, Luxembourg on GDP per \
capita) produce enormous per-cell densities that can destabilise the \
advection. ",
 StyleBox["WorldCartogram", "Input"],
 " clamps values at a configurable fraction of the mean and caps the \
rasterised grid at a multiple of the positive-cell mean.\n\[Bullet] ",
 "Long country edges are densified before being transformed, matching ",
 StyleBox["shapely.segmentize", "Input"],
 " on the Python side of this project.\n\[Bullet] ",
 "The algorithm is grid-convention-compatible with the Python implementation \
so the two are drop-in interchangeable for debugging / cross-validation."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"64ea1bc5-4cec-49fd-802f-ec0c2ac91e42"],

Cell["9. Related work in the Wolfram ecosystem", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"9b6cf126-e674-43c0-84b1-3f05fe03462f"],

Cell["\<\
This is not the first Wolfram-Language treatment of cartograms. Two earlier \
resources are worth reading alongside this one:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"ac33a53a-cc9f-4e34-9be3-d6e648c1f227"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\[Bullet] The 2019 Wolfram Community post ",
 ButtonBox["Creating a contiguous cartogram for the US",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://community.wolfram.com/groups/-/m/t/1729609"], None}],
 " by George Varnavides presents a ",
 StyleBox["Manipulate", "Input"],
 "-driven US-state cartogram with an interactive colour legend, originally \
written for the 2019 Wolfram Summer School. It focuses on a single country, \
uses an iterative scaling update rather than diffusion, and is a nice \
demonstration of how much can be done inside a notebook with no package at \
all."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"b9ac7443-f0c9-4c63-8fbc-38d9a81ac2bf"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\[Bullet] ",
 StyleBox["ResourceFunction[\"Cartogram\"]", "Input"],
 " \[LongDash] ",
 ButtonBox["resources.wolframcloud.com/FunctionRepository/resources/Cartogram",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://resources.wolframcloud.com/FunctionRepository/resources/\
Cartogram"], None}],
 " \[LongDash] is a function-repository entry that produces a contiguous \
cartogram directly from an ",
 StyleBox["EntityClass", "Input"],
 " plus a property. It is a single-call convenience similar in spirit to ",
 StyleBox["WorldCartogram", "Input"],
 ", runs inside the Wolfram Cloud with no installation, and is the right tool \
when you need a quick cartogram without thinking about parameters."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"461e9bcf-6902-4fd6-98d4-b3929230d01a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Relative to those two, the contribution of the present package is (a) a \
pure-Wolfram port of the full Gastner\[Dash]Newman diffusion algorithm \
exposed as a handful of composable primitives (",
 StyleBox["DiffusionSolver", "Input"],
 ", ",
 StyleBox["AdvectPoints", "Input"],
 ", ",
 StyleBox["RasterizePolygons", "Input"],
 ", \[Ellipsis]) that can be used on any rectangular domain, not just a \
country set; (b) built-in support for ocean-area preservation, user-supplied ",
 StyleBox["Association", "Input"],
 " input, and satellite-textured rendering; and (c) a default ",
 StyleBox["PerformanceGoal -> \"Speed\"", "Input"],
 " mode that cuts a full world cartogram from ~5 minutes to under a minute \
while staying sub-pixel-accurate."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"6afcef8a-2b9e-41ea-8d52-b66f9cdfc6fd"],

Cell["10. References", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"b912117e-be4e-4e7b-9394-859e8bb8a096"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\[Bullet] M. T. Gastner & M. E. J. Newman, \"Diffusion-based method for \
producing density-equalizing maps\", ",
 StyleBox["PNAS",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " ",
 StyleBox["101",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 "(20), 7499\[Dash]7504 (2004). ",
 ButtonBox["doi:10.1073/pnas.0400280101",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0400280101"], None}],
 ".\n\[Bullet] ",
 "Source code and the Python twin:\n        ",
 ButtonBox["github.com/mthiel74/Contiguous-Cartograms",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://github.com/mthiel74/Contiguous-Cartograms"], None}],
 ".\n\[Bullet] ",
 "Relies on Wolfram's ",
 StyleBox["CountryData", "Input"],
 ", ",
 StyleBox["GeoGraphics", "Input"],
 ", ",
 StyleBox["FourierDCT", "Input"],
 ", and ",
 StyleBox["NDSolveValue", "Input"],
 "."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"8926f8a3-802b-4642-8582-25d6329b1448"]
},
